Subject: [PATCH] usb: gadget: f_fs: pass through vendor descriptors on
 vendor-class interfaces

SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only

ffs_do_single_desc() rejects any descriptor type it does not model, and a
single rejection fails the entire descriptor set. This makes functionfs
unusable for devices whose interface must carry a vendor-defined descriptor.

The Xbox 360 controller and wireless receiver are the motivating case. They
carry Microsoft's XUSB descriptor, type 0x21 (wired) or 0x22 (adapter), on an
interface of class 0xFF, subclass 0x5D. Type 0x21 lands in the
USB_TYPE_CLASS | 0x01 case and is rejected there because the interface class is
not HID, CCID or DFU; type 0x22 reaches the default case. Without that
descriptor Windows' xusb22 driver will not instantiate a controller, so the
device enumerates but is never usable as a gamepad.

Accept both types verbatim, scoped to vendor-specific interfaces, so no
standard class parsing behaviour changes.

Tested on a Raspberry Pi (dwc2) presenting an Xbox 360 Wireless Receiver to
Windows: the controller is created, XInput input is delivered, and output
reports (rumble, LED ring) arrive on the OUT endpoint.

---
--- a/drivers/usb/gadget/function/f_fs.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/gadget/function/f_fs.c
@@ -2535,6 +2535,20 @@
 		}							\
 	} while (0)
 
+	/* Vendor-specific interfaces may legitimately carry descriptor types
+	 * this parser does not model - the Xbox 360 XUSB descriptors 0x21
+	 * (wired) and 0x22 (adapter) are the case in point. Upstream rejects
+	 * them, which kills the ENTIRE descriptor set, so functionfs cannot be
+	 * used for an XInput device at all. Accept them verbatim, but ONLY on
+	 * a vendor-specific interface, so no standard class parsing changes.
+	 */
+	if (*current_class == 0xFF &&
+	    (_ds->bDescriptorType == 0x21 || _ds->bDescriptorType == 0x22)) {
+		pr_vdebug("vendor passthrough descriptor: %d\n",
+			  _ds->bDescriptorType);
+		return length;
+	}
+
 	/* Parse descriptor depending on type. */
 	switch (_ds->bDescriptorType) {
 	case USB_DT_DEVICE:
